#!/usr/bin/env bash
# shellcheck source-path=SCRIPTDIR
#
# THE LINE ABOVE IS LOAD-BEARING, AND ITS POSITION IS PART OF IT (.github#1718). This file `source`s
# two libraries out of `lib/`, and the `# shellcheck source=...` pragmas on those lines are RELATIVE.
# Without a `source-path`, shellcheck resolves them against ITS OWN working directory rather than this
# script's, so `shellcheck -x scripts/skill-view` run from the repository root — which is how every
# gate in this org invokes it, and how a developer types it — finds neither library and lints this
# file with BOTH sources unread, inventing SC2034s for every variable they define:
#
#   scripts/skill-view:NN:3: note: Not following: lib/args.sh:  … does not exist [SC1091]
#   scripts/skill-view:NN:3: note: Not following: lib/roots.sh: … does not exist [SC1091]
#
# `SCRIPTDIR` makes them resolve against the directory holding THIS FILE, so the command reproduces
# from any cwd — in CI, in a receiver's materialized tree, and on the desk of whoever runs shellcheck
# by hand. Fixing it with `-P`/`--source-path` on a gate's command line would fix the gate only, and
# leave the by-hand run broken; the pragma belongs in the file.
#
# IT MUST STAY ABOVE THE FIRST COMMAND. Measured with the pinned shellcheck 0.11.0: this directive is
# file-scoped only where it precedes every command in the file. Moved down next to the `source` lines
# it binds to THE NEXT COMMAND ONLY — it clears the `lib/args.sh` finding and leaves the `lib/roots.sh`
# one standing. Do not "tidy" it down to the code it describes.
#
# `scripts/skill-view` is a kit-materialized file (registry/repos.yml `kit:` row `skill-view`), so a
# receiver cannot repair this in its own tree: the next FsggKitMaterialize reverts the edit. That is
# why the fix is here and shipped as a kit release.
#
# skill-view — the checkout-time generated skill view, and the LOUD ABSENCE CHECK it makes mandatory.
#
# ADR-0067 (`resolve, don't copy`) §6/§7/§8; phase 2 of the rewrite decided on .github#1611, tracked as
# .github#1635. Phase 1 (.github#1621) measured the ground this stands on.
#
# THREE SUBCOMMANDS, AND THE SECOND IS THE REASON THE FIRST IS ALLOWED TO EXIST.
#
#   skill-view generate  produce each runtime's skill root as a VIEW of ONE source directory.
#   skill-view check     assert the skills that source declares are VISIBLE at each runtime's
#                        expected path — and FAIL LOUDLY when they are not.
#   skill-view roots     report the resolved roots and their filesystem classes, without requiring
#                        an expected skill source.
#   skill-view selftest  demonstrate that every lane of `check` CAN FIRE, over fixture trees.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE TWO LANES `check` GREW, AND WHY THEY ARE HERE RATHER THAN IN SEVEN RECEIVERS (.github#1710)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ADR-0067 §9 phase 4 retired every receiver's second COMMITTED skill root, turning it into a view
# root. §8 forbids a retirement trading a loud failure for a quiet one, so each of the seven
# receivers shipped a replacement alarm in its own retirement change. Measured 2026-07-28, that is
# SEVEN implementations of ONE invariant — `scripts/check-skill-view-roots.sh` at 23,050 / 21,353 /
# 18,346 / 18,277 / 14,864 bytes on SDD / Audio / Rendering / Game / Governance, plus
# `tests/composition/skill-view-roots.sh` on Net and `tests/composition/lib/skill-view-roots.sh` on
# Templates. A 55% size spread across files asserting the same thing, all written in one day, by a
# phase whose stated purpose is removing hand-copies. ADR-0067 §3's rule — one implementation per
# invariant, with a callable seam — applied where the ADR did not anticipate needing it.
#
# The seven did NOT build a parallel delivery mechanism; they bolted two lanes onto one that already
# existed (`scripts/skill-view` is a kit-delivered source, and `FsggKitCheckSkillView` is
# `AfterTargets="FsggKitMaterialize"` in every receiver). They bolted them on because `check` did not
# cover them. It does now:
#
#   LANE 1 — THE ROOTS DECLARATION (`--receiver-proj`). With `<FsggKitViewSkillRoots>` EMPTIED,
#   every kit gate is green and the root is simply gone from the runtime contract. Measured on
#   FS.GG.Net's and FS.GG.Audio's own trees, 2026-07-28:
#       dotnet build … -t:FsggKitMaterialize  -> "no view skill roots declared … nothing to assert."
#       coordination-sync --check --against-pin -> "OK — all 28 file(s) match the FS.GG.Kit … pins."
#   Both green, and Codex then resolves ZERO skills and exits 0 saying nothing. `coordination-sync`
#   reads `FsggKitSkillRoots` ALONE by design (#1584); `CheckSkillView` returns true on an empty
#   `FsggKitViewSkillRoots`. Nothing covered a view root's MEMBERSHIP. This lane does: the sorted
#   UNION of the two properties must equal the declared runtime root set.
#
#   LANE 2 — THE VIEW ACTUALLY RESOLVES. Already `check`'s subject (`classify_root` below), and
#   already STRONGER than all seven hand-copies: they compare DIRECTORY COUNTS
#   (`find … -type d | wc -l`), which passes a view whose two skills are named nothing like the live
#   root's two; `check` compares per-skill IDENTITY at `<id>/SKILL.md`. Two of the seven — Rendering
#   and Governance — also guard absence with a bare `[[ ! -e ]]`, which FOLLOWS symlinks, leaving
#   their dangling-link branch unreachable and the class misattributed. `classify_root` has always
#   separated the four classes. The collapse therefore fixes both by construction rather than by
#   repair.
#
# WHAT IS GENUINELY PER-RECEIVER, AND IS A PARAMETER RATHER THAN A COPY:
#
#   * THE ABSENT-VIEW-ROOT POLICY (`--absent-ok`). An absent view root is RED by default and that
#     stays the default. But a receiver whose alarm runs on a BARE checkout — before any materialize,
#     which is the normal state of that job — must treat absence as green or the alarm fires on every
#     green build. That was the honest, measured split across the seven: Audio and Game ran on bare
#     checkouts (absent -> green); Rendering, SDD and Governance ran `skill-view generate` first
#     (absent -> red). It is a boolean, and it is NOT free: `--absent-ok` REQUIRES a reason, printed
#     on every run, because what else catches an absent root DIFFERS PER RECEIVER and must be
#     measured rather than assumed. On Game the kit's own check reds a required job (its alarm rides
#     the same job that materializes); on Audio it does NOT, because `kit / coordination-kit` grades
#     `<FsggKitSkillRoots>` bytes only and the materialize job is not required. A receiver that
#     cannot state what covers absence has not earned the carve-out.
#
#   * THE REQUIRED-CONTEXT NAME the alarm rides is per-receiver by construction and is NOT a
#     parameter of this tool — it is the workflow step that invokes it. All seven differ (Game rides
#     `Build-config drift check`, Governance and SDD ride `skill-view-check`, Rendering rides
#     `Deterministic gate`, Audio rides `Build + test`, Templates rides `composition`, Net rides an
#     unrequired `Composition (runtime skill roots)` — .github#1727). One line in a gate.yml is not a
#     hand-copy of an invariant.
#
# ONE LANE WAS ADOPTED FROM A RECEIVER RATHER THAN IMPOSED ON IT. FS.GG.Net's copy carried an
# assertion no other copy had — that git does not TRACK content under a view root — catching a
# re-committed view root, which resolves perfectly on Linux and passes every other copy's resolution
# lane while being exactly the committed symlink ADR-0067 §6 rejects on measurement. It is folded in
# for all receivers rather than dropped to make the collapse tidy (#1710: do not weaken any alarm to
# make the collapse easier). `generate` has always refused to write over a root git tracks
# (ADR-0067 §9); this asserts the same invariant on the checking side.
#
# EVERY LANE DEMONSTRATES IT CAN FIRE, AND THE DEMONSTRATION IS ITSELF MUTATION-TESTED —
# `skill-view selftest`. This is not ceremony; it is the whole reason the hole existed. FS.GG.Audio's
# alarm guarded absence with `[[ ! -e ]]` and shipped GREEN on a dangling view root — ADR-0067 §8's
# HEADLINE class — for a day, with the branch naming it unreachable, because that lane had no demo
# while the lane beside it in the same file had one and was correct (FS.GG.Audio#212, fixed in
# `26d2bb7`). `selftest` asserts the SPECIFIC diagnostic class each branch emits, never merely that
# something failed: a demo that only counts failures survives a mutation collapsing two branches into
# one. It drives the real `check` as a SUBPROCESS over fixture trees, which also sidesteps the hazard
# FS.GG.SDD's copy documents — an in-process demo capturing output through `$( )` runs the assertion
# in a SUBSHELL, discarding the counters it increments, and reports "nothing fired" while looking
# exactly like one that works.
#
# WHY `check` IS NOT OPTIONAL AND NOT DEFERRABLE (ADR-0067 §8). Today a materialization bug turns a
# gate red: the copied tree fails visibly. Under resolution every failure mode phase 1 measured —
# an ABSENT root, a DANGLING symlink, and a `core.symlinks=false` checkout of a COMMITTED symlink —
# is **exit 0 with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes** (Claude Code 2.1.220, Codex CLI 0.145.0), and the
# result is an agent that quietly has no skills. Shipping `generate` without `check` would trade a
# loud failure for a silent one, which ADR-0067 records in as many words: *"a rewrite that removes the
# loud failure and adds the quiet one is worse than no rewrite."* So `generate` RUNS `check` over what
# it just produced, and refuses to report success without it.
#
# WHY NOT A COMMITTED SYMLINK (ADR-0067 §6). A committed symlink does not DEGRADE under
# `git -c core.symlinks=false` — the git-for-Windows default without Developer Mode. It checks out as a
# small regular TEXT FILE whose body is the link target, and both runtimes then exit 0 with zero skills.
# ADR-0011's and ADR-0014 Decision 6's rejection of committed symlinks stands. This tool emits nothing
# tracked; `git status --porcelain` is empty after it runs. It links where it can and copies where it
# must, and neither runtime can tell the difference (phase 1 Q3, measured).
#
# WHY NOT A RESOLVER (ADR-0067 §7). `--plugin-dir`, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, `CODEX_HOME` and the local
# directory marketplace all work, and each costs session-only scope, a relocated config home including
# auth, or a per-machine bootstrap. Producing the directory at the runtime's own expected path is
# strictly cheaper than every one of them.
#
# THIS TOOL RETIRES NOTHING (ADR-0067 §9). `coordination-sync`, kit materialization,
# `kit-published-coherence`, `coordination-coherence`, `skill-union-assert` and the freshness sweep keep
# running unchanged. `generate` REFUSES a root that git TRACKS, with no override — a repo that still
# commits its roots is a repo whose replacement is not yet proven, and a generator able to delete
# committed skills there would be executing phase 4 by accident. Untracking a root is a decision, taken
# in the change that proves the view, never a side effect of running this.
#
# THE ROOT SET IS NOT THIS TOOL'S TO CHOOSE. It comes from `lib/roots.sh` — the SAME precedence parser
# `skill-union-assert.sh` and `coordination-sync` resolve with (.github#525), because a tree whose root
# set has two readers that agree only by coincidence of defaults is the defect that library exists to
# have ended. ADR-0067 §5's two-root end state is a separate, separately-authorised change
# (.github#1636); nothing here anticipates it.
#
# THE EXPECTED SET IS NEVER DERIVED FROM THE ROOTS THEMSELVES. `check` demands `--source` or
# `--manifest` and dies without one. Deriving "what should be visible" by looking at what IS visible is
# the vacuous-pass shape (epic #266): every root empty would then satisfy every root. An expected set
# that comes out EMPTY is likewise exit 2, never a pass — "I found nothing to look for" and "everything
# I looked for is there" are opposite facts.
#
# BEWARE THE SUBSET MANIFEST. `--manifest` believes what it is given. `registry/coordination-kit-
# skill-manifest.json` declares FOUR of this repo's thirteen skills and `registry/driver-skill-
# manifest.json` declares six; either would pass over a tree that had lost the other nine. That is
# .github#1504's coherent-but-incomplete class exactly. Under the resolved layout the honest expectation
# is the SOURCE directory, which cannot be a subset of itself.
#
# Usage:
#   skill-view generate (--source <dir> | --receiver-proj <file>) [--tree <dir>]
#                       [--roots "<r1> <r2> ..."] [--mode auto|link|copy]
#   skill-view check    (--source <dir> | --manifest <file.json>) [--tree <dir>] [--roots "<r1> ..."]
#                       [--receiver-proj <file>] [--expect-roots "<r1> ..."] [--absent-ok "<why>"]
#   skill-view roots    [--tree <dir>] [--roots "<r1> ..."]
#   skill-view selftest [--tree <dir>]
#
# Exit: 0 = every declared skill is visible at every configured root
#       1 = a VERDICT: at least one skill is not visible somewhere (each printed with its class)
#       2 = misconfiguration, or no verdict could be reached. Never a pass.
#
# BOTH 1 AND 2 ARE RED, AND NEITHER IS SOFTER THAN THE OTHER. The split says WHAT KIND of answer
# this is — a finding about the tree, or an admission that no finding could be reached — because
# "I could not evaluate this" is never "I evaluated it and it passed" (.github#266). A caller that
# treats exit 2 as a warning has reintroduced the fail-open this whole file exists to prevent.

set -euo pipefail

TREE="."
ROOTS=""
ROOTS_ARG=""
ROOTS_SRC=""
SOURCE=""
MANIFEST=""
MODE="auto"

# --- the roots-declaration lane's state (.github#1710 lane 1) -----------------------------------
RECEIVER_PROJ=""       # the receiver project both root properties live in
EXPECT_ROOTS=""        # the runtime root set the union must equal (--expect-roots)
VIEW_ROOTS=""          # the subset of ROOTS that are VIEW roots, from <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>
LIVE_ROOTS=""          # the subset that are materialized-into roots, from <FsggKitSkillRoots>
MEMBERSHIP_FAULT=""    # non-empty when the declaration could not be read: a VERDICT, not a skip
ABSENT_OK_WHY=""       # --absent-ok's mandatory reason; empty means absent view roots stay RED

# ADR-0065's two, as amended by ADR-0067 §5 and landed by .github#1636. `lib/roots.sh` takes the
# default from its CALLER precisely so a migration changes it in one reviewed place rather than four
# by drift — and this is that migration: `.codex/skills` is Codex's OTHER native root, so it carried
# no runtime the remaining two do not and only ever produced a duplicate catalog entry.
DEFAULT_ROOTS=".claude/skills .agents/skills"
DEFAULT_LABEL="default (ADR-0065's two)"

die() { echo "::error::skill-view: $*" >&2; exit 2; }

# shellcheck source=lib/args.sh
. "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/lib/args.sh"
# shellcheck source=lib/roots.sh
. "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/lib/roots.sh"

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
skill-view — generate a runtime skill root as a view of one source, and assert it is visible.

  skill-view generate (--source <dir> | --receiver-proj <file>) [--tree <dir>]
                      [--roots "<r1> <r2>"] [--mode auto|link|copy]
  skill-view check    (--source <dir> | --manifest <file.json>) [--tree <dir>] [--roots "<r1> <r2>"]
                      [--receiver-proj <file>] [--expect-roots "<r1> <r2>"] [--absent-ok "<why>"]
  skill-view selftest [--tree <dir>]

  --source <dir>     the ONE source of truth: a directory of <id>/SKILL.md skills.
  --manifest <file>  (check only) a JSON catalog {"skills":[{"id":...}]} to expect instead.
  --tree <dir>       the repository root the roots are relative to (default: .).
  --roots "<...>"    override the root set. Precedence: --roots > $AGENT_SKILL_ROOTS >
                     <tree>/.agent-skill-roots > the ADR-0011 default.
  --mode             auto (symlink, fall back to copy), link (symlink or die), copy (always copy).

  --receiver-proj <file>   for check, grade the ROOTS DECLARATION too: read <FsggKitSkillRoots>
                     and <FsggKitViewSkillRoots> from an FS.GG.Kit receiver project and assert their
                     sorted UNION equals --expect-roots. Also supplies the root set to resolve (so
                     --roots is refused alongside it), marks which roots are VIEW roots, and enables
                     the tracked-view-root assertion. This is ADR-0067 §8's membership half: with
                     <FsggKitViewSkillRoots> emptied, every other kit gate is green.
                     For generate, the same declaration supplies the ONE live source root and the
                     VIEW roots to write, so a workflow cannot hand-copy a different pair. It is
                     refused with --source or --roots: the declaration is the whole input set.
  --expect-roots "<...>"   the runtime root set the union must equal. Default: ADR-0011's two.
                     Set equality, not a minimum — ADR-0065 governs ADDING a root exactly as it
                     governs removing one, and a legal disposition swap between the two properties
                     leaves the union unchanged and must stay green.
  --absent-ok "<why>"      an ABSENT view root is green rather than red, for an alarm that runs on a
                     bare checkout where an ungenerated view is the normal state. The reason is
                     REQUIRED and is printed on every run: what else catches an absent root differs
                     per receiver and must be MEASURED, not assumed (.github#1710).

Exit: 0 ok; 1 a verdict (a skill not visible, or the declaration wrong); 2 misconfiguration / no
      verdict. BOTH 1 AND 2 ARE RED — see the note at the top of this file.
EOF
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared helpers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

need_cmd() { command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "$1 not found ($2)."; }

abs() { (cd "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd) || die "not a directory: $1"; }

# ids_from_source <dir> — the skills a source directory declares: every immediate subdirectory that
# holds a SKILL.md. A subdirectory WITHOUT one is not a skill and is not silently counted as one.
ids_from_source() {
  local src="$1" d
  for d in "$src"/*/; do
    [ -d "$d" ] || continue
    [ -f "$d/SKILL.md" ] || continue
    basename "$d"
  done
}

# ids_from_manifest <file> — `.skills[].id`, read with python3 rather than a regex, because a catalog
# read wrongly is a catalog that certifies the wrong set.
ids_from_manifest() {
  need_cmd python3 "required to read --manifest"
  python3 - "$1" <<'PY'
import json, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
try:
    doc = json.loads(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read())
except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
    sys.exit(f"cannot read manifest {path}: {e}")
skills = doc.get("skills")
if not isinstance(skills, list):
    sys.exit(f"{path}: .skills must be an array")
for row in skills:
    if not isinstance(row, dict) or not str(row.get("id", "")).strip():
        sys.exit(f"{path}: every .skills[] row needs a non-empty id")
    print(row["id"])
PY
}

# classify_root <abs-root-path> — WHAT IS ACTUALLY AT THE RUNTIME'S EXPECTED PATH.
#
# The three classes below are the three failure modes phase 1 measured as exit-0-and-silent in both
# runtimes. They are reported SEPARATELY on purpose: `skill-union-assert.sh` calls all three
# "configured root is absent … this is a REAL partition: the producer never materialized <root>",
# which is measurably wrong for two of them — the path is right there, and no producer was involved.
# A diagnostic that names the wrong cause sends the reader to the wrong repair, which is the failure
# `lib/roots.sh`'s own CRLF note warns about one layer down.
classify_root() {
  local p="$1"
  if [ -d "$p" ]; then echo "dir"; return; fi
  if [ -L "$p" ]; then echo "dangling-link"; return; fi     # a link, and -d already said it resolves nowhere
  if [ -e "$p" ]; then
    if [ -f "$p" ] && looks_like_link_body "$p"; then echo "text-file-link"; return; fi
    echo "not-a-directory"; return
  fi
  echo "absent"
}

# do_roots — expose classify_root to callers that must judge the roots before a readable source
# exists.  Keep this deliberately source-free: the hook's subject can itself be the source.
do_roots() {
  local root cls
  for root in $ROOTS; do
    cls="$(classify_root "$TREE_ABS/$root")"
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$root" "$cls"
  done
}

# looks_like_link_body <file> — is this regular file the CHECKED-OUT BODY of a committed symlink?
#
# Two independent tells, strongest first:
#   1. git records the path with mode 120000 (a symlink) while the working tree holds a regular file.
#      That is `core.symlinks=false` and nothing else, and it needs no guessing.
#   2. failing a git answer: a short, single-line, NUL-free body that names a path. This is a SNIFF and
#      says so in its message — it exists so the class is still named on a tree git cannot speak for
#      (an export, a tarball, a vendored copy).
looks_like_link_body() {
  local f="$1" mode body
  if [ -n "${TREE_ABS:-}" ] && git -C "$TREE_ABS" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    mode="$(git -C "$TREE_ABS" ls-files --stage -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1; exit}')"
    [ "$mode" = "120000" ] && return 0
  fi
  [ "$(wc -c <"$f")" -le 1024 ] || return 1
  # A NUL byte means a binary blob, not a link body. Tested by DELETION rather than by `grep -P`,
  # whose PCRE mode is a GNU extension this repo cannot assume on every runner.
  [ "$(LC_ALL=C tr -d '\000' <"$f" | wc -c)" -eq "$(wc -c <"$f")" ] || return 1
  [ "$(wc -l <"$f")" -le 1 ] || return 1
  body="$(tr -d '\n\r' <"$f")"
  case "$body" in
    "" ) return 1 ;;
    */* ) return 0 ;;
    * ) [ -e "$(dirname "$f")/$body" ] && return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LANE 1 — THE ROOTS DECLARATION (.github#1710 piece 2)
#
# IT GRADES THE DECLARATION, NOT MSBUILD'S EVALUATION, and that is deliberate rather than lazy. The
# faithful alternative is `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:` on the receiver project, which needs a
# RESTORE of the pinned FS.GG.Kit — a network round-trip added to a REQUIRED check to grade a
# two-line fact the receiver authors in its own tree. It would also introduce a second source of
# truth for the package's defaults: a property a receiver does NOT declare evaluates to the package
# default, so a text reader would have to restate `.claude/skills;.agents/skills` to interpret an
# absence, and a restated default is the invented-location bug one file over. Requiring BOTH
# properties to be declared EXPLICITLY removes the question: an absence is a RED, not a guess.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# msbuild_property <file> <name> — the text of a single-line `<name>value</name>` element. Echoes
# nothing and returns 1 when the element is absent, empty, or not on one line. Deliberately NOT an
# XML parser: the one thing this needs to distinguish is "declared with a value" from "anything
# else", and every "anything else" lands on the same red. A declaration this cannot read is a
# declaration a reviewer should reformat.
msbuild_property() {
  local file="$1" name="$2" value
  [ -r "$file" ] || return 1
  value="$(sed -n "s|^[[:space:]]*<${name}>\(.*\)</${name}>[[:space:]]*\$|\1|p" "$file" | head -1)"
  [ -n "$value" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$value"
}

# normalize_roots <text> — split on ';' and whitespace, strip trailing slashes, drop empties, sort
# unique, join with single spaces. SORTED, so a comparison is set equality and not an accident of
# which property each root is declared in: moving a root between the two properties is a legal
# ADR-0065 disposition change and must NOT red the membership lane.
normalize_roots() {
  printf '%s' "$1" | tr ';' '\n' | tr -s ' \t' '\n' \
    | sed 's|[[:space:]]||g; s|/*$||' | grep -v '^$' | sort -u | paste -sd' ' - || true
}

# read_receiver_proj <file> — set LIVE_ROOTS / VIEW_ROOTS / ROOTS from a receiver project, or set
# MEMBERSHIP_FAULT explaining why not. An UNDECLARED property must never read as an empty
# contribution to the union: that would make deleting it the very thing it silently allows.
read_receiver_proj() {
  local file="$1" live view
  if [ ! -r "$file" ]; then
    MEMBERSHIP_FAULT="cannot read the receiver project '$file'. \"I could not look\" is never \"looked, and fine\" (.github#266)."
    return
  fi
  if ! live="$(msbuild_property "$file" FsggKitSkillRoots)"; then
    MEMBERSHIP_FAULT="'$file' does not declare <FsggKitSkillRoots> on one line with a value. Both root properties must be declared EXPLICITLY — an absence is a RED, not the package default silently restated here."
    return
  fi
  if ! view="$(msbuild_property "$file" FsggKitViewSkillRoots)"; then
    MEMBERSHIP_FAULT="'$file' does not declare <FsggKitViewSkillRoots> on one line with a value. THIS IS THE REGRESSION THIS LANE EXISTS FOR: with it emptied or deleted, the kit materialize reports 'no view skill roots declared … nothing to assert' and coordination-sync reports OK, because it grades <FsggKitSkillRoots> bytes only (#1584) — both GREEN while the root leaves the runtime contract and the runtime resolves ZERO skills, exiting 0 saying nothing (ADR-0067 §8)."
    return
  fi
  LIVE_ROOTS="$(normalize_roots "$live")"
  VIEW_ROOTS="$(normalize_roots "$view")"
  ROOTS="$(normalize_roots "$live;$view")"
  ROOTS_SRC="$(basename "$file") (<FsggKitSkillRoots> ∪ <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>)"
}

# is_view_root <root> — is this root one the receiver declared as a VIEW root? The absent-ok policy
# and the tracked assertion apply to view roots ONLY. A materialized root that is absent is always a
# red: the kit wrote it, so its absence is drift rather than a bare checkout.
is_view_root() {
  local r="$1" v
  for v in $VIEW_ROOTS; do [ "$v" = "$r" ] && return 0; done
  return 1
}

# assert_root_membership — the union of the two properties IS the runtime root set. Echoes nothing
# and returns 0 on a pass; prints the violation and returns 1 otherwise.
assert_root_membership() {
  local want
  if [ -n "$MEMBERSHIP_FAULT" ]; then
    echo "::error::skill-view: [roots-declaration] $MEMBERSHIP_FAULT" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  want="$(normalize_roots "$EXPECT_ROOTS")"
  if [ "$ROOTS" = "$want" ]; then
    echo "  roots-declaration: the runtime skill root set is '$ROOTS' — the union of <FsggKitSkillRoots> ('$LIVE_ROOTS') and <FsggKitViewSkillRoots> ('$VIEW_ROOTS')"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "::error::skill-view: [roots-declaration] this tree's runtime skill roots are '$ROOTS', not '$want'. A root that leaves this union leaves the runtime contract, and every other gate stays green while it does: coordination-sync reads <FsggKitSkillRoots> alone (#1584), and FsggKitCheckSkillView reports 'nothing to assert' for an empty <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>. The runtime would then resolve zero skills and exit 0 saying nothing (ADR-0067 §8). If the root set is genuinely meant to change, that is an ADR-0065 'Retiring a root' contract migration — amend the record and the caller's --expect-roots in the SAME change." >&2
  return 1
}

# assert_view_roots_untracked — a VIEW root git TRACKS is a committed view, which is the committed
# symlink ADR-0067 §6 rejects on measurement: under `git -c core.symlinks=false` (the git-for-Windows
# default without Developer Mode) it checks out as a small regular TEXT FILE whose body is the link
# target, and both runtimes then exit 0 with zero skills. Adopted from FS.GG.Net's copy, which was
# the only one of the seven that asserted it, and folded in for every receiver rather than dropped to
# make the collapse tidy. `generate` has always REFUSED to write over a tracked root (ADR-0067 §9);
# this is the same invariant on the checking side, where a re-commit would otherwise resolve
# perfectly on Linux and pass every resolution lane.
assert_view_roots_untracked() {
  local root tracked n rc=0
  [ -n "$VIEW_ROOTS" ] || return 0
  if ! git -C "$TREE_ABS" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # Not a pass. Said out loud, because a lane that silently grades nothing is the shape #266 is
    # about — and this one legitimately cannot run on an export, a tarball or a fixture tree.
    echo "  view-root-tracking: NOT GRADED — '$TREE_ABS' is not a git work tree, so git could not be asked whether the view roots are tracked. Nothing is asserted about it."
    return 0
  fi
  for root in $VIEW_ROOTS; do
    tracked="$(git -C "$TREE_ABS" ls-files -- "$root" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [ -n "$tracked" ]; then
      n="$(printf '%s\n' "$tracked" | grep -c . || true)"
      echo "::error::skill-view: [tracked-view-root] '$root' is declared a VIEW root but git TRACKS $n path(s) under it. Either the retirement was reverted — fine, but then move the root back to <FsggKitSkillRoots> so coordination-sync grades it again — or a generated view was COMMITTED, which is the committed symlink ADR-0067 §6 rejects: it checks out as a plain text file under core.symlinks=false and both runtimes then resolve zero skills silently. \`generate\` refuses to write over this root while it stays tracked." >&2
      rc=1
    fi
  done
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && echo "  view-root-tracking: git tracks nothing under $(printf '%s' "$VIEW_ROOTS" | wc -w) declared view root(s)"
  return "$rc"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check — THE LOUD ABSENCE CHECK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
do_check() {
  local ids=() id root p cls violations=0 checked=0 visible root_ok

  # LANE 1 FIRST, AND ITS FAULT IS FATAL BEFORE THE ROOTS LOOP. When the declaration could not be
  # read there is no honest root set to resolve against, so reporting "0 violations" over a set this
  # tool invented would be the vacuous pass (#266) wearing the membership lane's clothes.
  if [ -n "$RECEIVER_PROJ" ]; then
    echo "skill-view check: grading the roots declaration in '$RECEIVER_PROJ' against '$EXPECT_ROOTS'"
    if ! assert_root_membership; then
      echo "::error::skill-view: the roots DECLARATION is wrong — refusing to go on and report on a root set this tree does not actually declare." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    assert_view_roots_untracked || violations=$((violations + 1))
  fi

  local raw=""
  if [ -n "$SOURCE" ]; then
    [ -d "$SOURCE" ] || die "--source is not a directory: $SOURCE"
    raw="$(ids_from_source "$SOURCE")"
  elif [ -n "$MANIFEST" ]; then
    [ -f "$MANIFEST" ] || die "--manifest is not a file: $MANIFEST"
    # A manifest this tool COULD NOT READ is exit 2, never an empty expected set: "unreadable" and
    # "declares nothing" are opposite facts and only one of them is safe to act on.
    raw="$(ids_from_manifest "$MANIFEST")" || die "could not read --manifest $MANIFEST (see above)."
  else
    die "check needs --source <dir> or --manifest <file>. Deriving the expected set from the roots would make every empty root pass (epic #266)."
  fi

  [ -n "$raw" ] || die "the expected skill set is EMPTY — refusing to report 'everything is visible' over nothing (epic #266)."
  mapfile -t ids <<<"$raw"
  [ "${#ids[@]}" -gt 0 ] || die "the expected skill set is EMPTY — refusing to report 'everything is visible' over nothing (epic #266)."

  local nroots=0
  for root in $ROOTS; do nroots=$((nroots + 1)); done

  echo "skill-view check: tree='$TREE_ABS' roots='$ROOTS' (from $ROOTS_SRC) expecting ${#ids[@]} skill(s)"

  for root in $ROOTS; do
    p="$TREE_ABS/$root"
    cls="$(classify_root "$p")"
    root_ok=0
    case "$cls" in
      dir) root_ok=1 ;;
      absent)
        # THE ONE SHAPE A CALLER MAY EXCUSE, AND ONLY FOR A VIEW ROOT, AND ONLY WITH A REASON.
        # A materialized root's absence is drift and stays red no matter what --absent-ok says.
        if [ -n "$ABSENT_OK_WHY" ] && [ -n "$RECEIVER_PROJ" ] && is_view_root "$root"; then
          echo "  $root: absent — accepted by --absent-ok: $ABSENT_OK_WHY"
          continue
        fi
        echo "::error::skill-view: [absent-root] $root does not exist. The view was never generated, so this runtime resolves ZERO skills and exits 0 while doing it. Run: scripts/skill-view generate --source <dir>" >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)) ;;
      dangling-link)
        echo "::error::skill-view: [dangling-root] $root is a symlink that resolves to nothing (-> $(readlink "$p")). This runtime resolves ZERO skills and exits 0 while doing it." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)) ;;
      text-file-link)
        echo "::error::skill-view: [text-file-root] $root is a regular FILE whose body names a path ('$(tr -d '\n\r' <"$p")'). That is a COMMITTED SYMLINK checked out with core.symlinks=false — the git-for-Windows default without Developer Mode. ADR-0067 §6: the mechanism is a generated view, never a committed symlink. This runtime resolves ZERO skills and exits 0 while doing it." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)) ;;
      *)
        echo "::error::skill-view: [not-a-directory-root] $root exists and is not a directory. This runtime resolves ZERO skills and exits 0 while doing it." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)) ;;
    esac

    if [ "$root_ok" -eq 0 ]; then
      # The per-skill loop below would report ${#ids[@]} identical follow-on failures for one cause.
      # One named cause is what a reader can act on; N derived ones are what they scroll past.
      continue
    fi

    visible=0
    for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
      checked=$((checked + 1))
      p="$TREE_ABS/$root/$id/SKILL.md"
      if [ ! -e "$p" ]; then
        if [ -L "$p" ]; then
          echo "::error::skill-view: [dangling-skill] $root/$id/SKILL.md is a symlink resolving to nothing (-> $(readlink "$p"))." >&2
        else
          echo "::error::skill-view: [missing-skill] $root/$id/SKILL.md is not visible at this runtime's expected path." >&2
        fi
        violations=$((violations + 1)); continue
      fi
      if [ ! -f "$p" ]; then
        echo "::error::skill-view: [not-a-file-skill] $root/$id/SKILL.md exists and is not a regular file." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)); continue
      fi
      if [ ! -r "$p" ]; then
        echo "::error::skill-view: [unreadable-skill] $root/$id/SKILL.md is not readable. A runtime that cannot read it has no skill; unreadable is not present." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)); continue
      fi
      if [ ! -s "$p" ]; then
        echo "::error::skill-view: [empty-skill] $root/$id/SKILL.md is empty." >&2
        violations=$((violations + 1)); continue
      fi
      visible=$((visible + 1))
    done
    echo "  $root: $visible/${#ids[@]} declared skill(s) visible"
  done

  if [ "$violations" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "::error::skill-view: $violations violation(s) — the declared skills are NOT all visible. Both runtimes would exit 0 over this tree; this check is the only thing that says so." >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  # The denominator is printed with the count, per .github#1513: a count whose population is invisible
  # is a coverage claim the reader supplies for themselves, generously.
  echo "skill-view check: OK — ${#ids[@]} declared skill(s) visible in every one of $nroots root(s) ($checked path(s) examined)."
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# generate — THE VIEW
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# relpath <from-dir> <to-path> — a relative symlink target, so the view survives a moved checkout.
relpath() {
  need_cmd python3 "required to compute a relative symlink target"
  python3 -c 'import os,sys; print(os.path.relpath(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[1]))' "$1" "$2"
}

do_generate() {
  local ids=() root dst parent target cls mode_used

  [ -n "$SOURCE" ] || die "generate needs --source <dir>: the ONE source of truth the view is a view OF."
  [ -d "$SOURCE" ] || die "--source is not a directory: $SOURCE"
  case "$MODE" in auto|link|copy) ;; *) die "--mode must be auto, link or copy (got '$MODE')." ;; esac

  local src_abs raw
  src_abs="$(abs "$SOURCE")"
  raw="$(ids_from_source "$src_abs")"
  [ -n "$raw" ] || die "--source '$SOURCE' holds no <id>/SKILL.md — refusing to generate an EMPTY view, which every runtime would accept in silence (epic #266)."
  mapfile -t ids <<<"$raw"

  echo "skill-view generate: tree='$TREE_ABS' source='$src_abs' (${#ids[@]} skill(s)) roots='$ROOTS' (from $ROOTS_SRC) mode=$MODE"

  for root in $ROOTS; do
    dst="$TREE_ABS/$root"

    # THE IDENTITY CASE IS ASKED FIRST, AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. ADR-0067 §5's end state makes
    # ONE root the canonical source — `.agents/skills`, because Codex reads it with no configuration —
    # and that root is TRACKED, because it is the source of truth. Asking the tracked question first
    # would refuse the very layout the record is aiming at. Generating "over" the source must never
    # remove it; there is nothing to do, and saying so is the whole answer.
    if [ -d "$dst" ] && [ "$(abs "$dst")" = "$src_abs" ]; then
      echo "  $root: identity (this root IS the source) — nothing to generate"
      continue
    fi

    # TRACKED ROOTS ARE REFUSED, WITH NO OVERRIDE (ADR-0067 §9). Nothing is retired before its
    # replacement is proven, and a generator that could clobber committed skills would retire them
    # by accident, in a repo whose CI is green, on a run nobody read.
    if git -C "$TREE_ABS" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      if [ -n "$(git -C "$TREE_ABS" ls-files -- "$root" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)" ]; then
        die "refusing to generate over '$root': git TRACKS content there. ADR-0067 §9 retires nothing before its replacement is proven — stop tracking the root in the change that proves the view, then generate. There is no --force for this."
      fi
    fi

    if [ -e "$dst" ] || [ -L "$dst" ]; then
      # Only a previously-generated view is replaced. A directory of unknown provenance is somebody's
      # data until proven otherwise.
      if [ -L "$dst" ]; then
        rm -f "$dst"
      elif [ -d "$dst" ] && [ -f "$dst/.skill-view" ]; then
        rm -rf "$dst"
      else
        die "refusing to replace '$root': it exists, is not a symlink, and carries no .skill-view receipt, so this tool did not create it."
      fi
    fi

    parent="$(dirname "$dst")"
    mkdir -p "$parent"

    mode_used=""
    if [ "$MODE" = "auto" ] || [ "$MODE" = "link" ]; then
      target="$(relpath "$parent" "$src_abs")"
      if ln -s "$target" "$dst" 2>/dev/null && [ -d "$dst" ]; then
        mode_used="link"
      else
        rm -f "$dst" 2>/dev/null || true
        if [ "$MODE" = "link" ]; then
          die "--mode link: could not create a usable symlink at '$root' (this filesystem or OS refuses one). Use --mode copy, or --mode auto."
        fi
      fi
    fi

    if [ -z "$mode_used" ]; then
      mkdir -p "$dst"
      # The receipt is what makes a re-run idempotent without a --force that could eat real data. It
      # is a DOTFILE at the root's top level, where neither runtime looks: both scan for
      # <id>/SKILL.md subdirectories, so it adds no catalog entry to either.
      printf 'generated by scripts/skill-view from %s\n' "$src_abs" > "$dst/.skill-view"
      for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
        cp -R "$src_abs/$id" "$dst/$id"
      done
      mode_used="copy"
    fi

    cls="$(classify_root "$dst")"
    [ "$cls" = "dir" ] || die "generated '$root' and it did not come out as a directory (classified: $cls). Refusing to report success."
    echo "  $root: generated ($mode_used)"

    # NOTHING IT EMITS IS TRACKED — and "untracked" is only half of it. A generated root that git does
    # not IGNORE shows up in `git status` and in every drift signal a worker reads, which is how a
    # generated artifact starts getting committed by someone tidying up. The remedy is a line in the
    # consuming repo's `.gitignore`, and this tool will not write one: a tracked file is exactly what
    # it may not produce. So it says so, loudly, and leaves the edit to the repo.
    if git -C "$TREE_ABS" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      if ! git -C "$TREE_ABS" check-ignore -q -- "$root" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "::warning::skill-view: '$root' is generated but NOT git-ignored — 'git status' will report it and someone will eventually commit it. Add '/$root' to the tree's .gitignore." >&2
      fi
    fi
  done

  # GENERATE RUNS CHECK. A view produced and not verified is exactly the silent failure this phase
  # exists to prevent, so the success path goes through the check or there is no success path.
  SOURCE="$src_abs"
  # Declaration-driven generation writes only VIEW roots, but its mandatory verification must grade
  # the complete runtime union.  Restoring the live roots here preserves the same declaration that
  # selected SOURCE above; it does not re-read or hand-copy workflow arguments.
  if [ -n "$RECEIVER_PROJ" ]; then
    ROOTS="$(normalize_roots "$LIVE_ROOTS;$VIEW_ROOTS")"
    ROOTS_SRC="$(basename "$RECEIVER_PROJ") (<FsggKitSkillRoots> ∪ <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>)"
    [ -n "$EXPECT_ROOTS" ] || EXPECT_ROOTS="$DEFAULT_ROOTS"
  fi
  do_check
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# selftest — EVERY LANE DEMONSTRATES IT CAN FIRE
#
# "Demonstrated, not asserted" (.github#1611 category D: a gate that never fires and a gate that
# always passes are indistinguishable from outside). Entirely offline, entirely local: fixture trees
# in a temp dir, driving the REAL `check` as a subprocess.
#
# THE DEMO IS ITSELF MUTATION-TESTED, AND THAT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A FLOURISH. Every negative
# case asserts the SPECIFIC diagnostic class its branch emits — `[dangling-root]`, `[text-file-root]`,
# `[absent-root]`, `[missing-skill]`, `[roots-declaration]`, `[tracked-view-root]` — never merely
# that the exit code was non-zero. A demo that only counts failures SURVIVES a mutation that
# collapses two branches into one, which is exactly how FS.GG.Audio's alarm shipped green on a
# dangling view root for a day (FS.GG.Audio#212): its `! -e` absence guard followed the symlink, the
# dangling case took the "fine" branch, and nothing in that file could tell. Here, collapsing
# `dangling-link` into `absent` reds case 8; making `absent` unconditionally green reds case 11;
# dropping the `--absent-ok` view-root scoping reds case 13. Each mutation was run before this
# landed and each named case failed, individually.
#
# RUNNING `check` AS A SUBPROCESS is also what makes the counters trustworthy. FS.GG.SDD's copy
# documents the hazard an in-process demo has to work around: capturing an assertion's output through
# `$( )` runs it in a SUBSHELL, so the PASS/FAIL counters it increments are discarded and every case
# reads "nothing fired" while looking exactly like one that works. A subprocess has no such shared
# state to lose — the verdict is the exit code and the evidence is the output.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SELFTEST_PASS=0
SELFTEST_FAIL=0

# mkskills <dir> <id...> — a source directory of <id>/SKILL.md skills.
mkskills() {
  local d="$1"; shift
  local id
  for id in "$@"; do
    mkdir -p "$d/$id"
    printf -- '---\nname: %s\n---\nfixture skill\n' "$id" > "$d/$id/SKILL.md"
  done
}

# mkproj <file> <live> <view> — a receiver project declaring the two root properties. An empty
# property writes the element with no content (the "emptied" regression); the literal string OMIT
# leaves that property out entirely (the "deleted" regression).
mkproj() {
  local f="$1" live="$2" view="$3"
  { printf '<Project>\n  <PropertyGroup>\n'
    [ "$live" = "OMIT" ] || printf '    <FsggKitSkillRoots>%s</FsggKitSkillRoots>\n' "$live"
    [ "$view" = "OMIT" ] || printf '    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>%s</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>\n' "$view"
    printf '  </PropertyGroup>\n</Project>\n'
  } > "$f"
}

# selftest_case <label> <expect> <args...>
#   expect = "ok"          -> the run must exit 0
#            "[some-class]" -> the run must exit non-zero AND print that exact class tag
selftest_case() {
  local label="$1" expect="$2"; shift 2
  local out rc=0
  out="$(bash "$SELF" check "$@" 2>&1)" || rc=$?

  if [ "$expect" = "ok" ]; then
    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
      SELFTEST_PASS=$((SELFTEST_PASS + 1)); printf '  ok    %s — exit 0\n' "$label"
    else
      SELFTEST_FAIL=$((SELFTEST_FAIL + 1))
      printf '  FAIL  %s — expected exit 0, got %s\n%s\n' "$label" "$rc" "$out" >&2
    fi
    return
  fi

  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
    SELFTEST_FAIL=$((SELFTEST_FAIL + 1))
    printf '  FAIL  %s — expected %s and a RED, got exit 0. This lane CANNOT FIRE.\n%s\n' \
      "$label" "$expect" "$out" >&2
    return
  fi
  case "$out" in
    *"$expect"*)
      SELFTEST_PASS=$((SELFTEST_PASS + 1)); printf '  ok    %s — RED with %s\n' "$label" "$expect" ;;
    *)
      # Non-zero for the WRONG reason is the misattribution bug two of the seven hand-copies shipped
      # (a bare `! -e` reporting a dangling link as absent). It fails here rather than passing.
      SELFTEST_FAIL=$((SELFTEST_FAIL + 1))
      printf '  FAIL  %s — RED (exit %s) but WITHOUT %s. The right class was not named.\n%s\n' \
        "$label" "$rc" "$expect" "$out" >&2 ;;
  esac
}

# selftest_generate_case is deliberately separate from selftest_case: the latter drives check,
# while this one proves generate's declaration reader and its refusal boundary.  Keeping both as
# subprocesses means the test observes the argv parser a workflow actually reaches.
selftest_generate_case() {
  local label="$1" expect="$2"; shift 2
  local out rc=0
  out="$(bash "$SELF" generate "$@" 2>&1)" || rc=$?
  case "$expect" in
    ok)
      if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "  PASS  $label"
        SELFTEST_PASS=$((SELFTEST_PASS + 1))
      else
        echo "  FAIL  $label — expected green, got exit $rc" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2
        SELFTEST_FAIL=$((SELFTEST_FAIL + 1))
      fi ;;
    *)
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && [[ "$out" == *"$expect"* ]]; then
        echo "  PASS  $label (red: $expect)"
        SELFTEST_PASS=$((SELFTEST_PASS + 1))
      else
        echo "  FAIL  $label — expected red containing '$expect', got exit $rc" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2
        SELFTEST_FAIL=$((SELFTEST_FAIL + 1))
      fi ;;
  esac
}

do_selftest() {
  local tmp t
  SELF="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2064  # expand tmp NOW: the trap must name this run's directory.
  trap "rm -rf '$tmp'" EXIT

  echo "skill-view selftest: every lane of 'check', demonstrated over fixture trees"
  echo
  echo "lane 1 — the roots declaration (.github#1710 piece 2)"

  # Both roots real, so the RESOLUTION lane is satisfied throughout and only the DECLARATION varies.
  mkdecl() {
    local t="$tmp/$1"
    mkdir -p "$t"
    mkskills "$t/.claude/skills" alpha beta
    mkskills "$t/.agents/skills" alpha beta
    printf '%s' "$t"
  }

  # A generate fixture starts with only the live source.  Its view root is absent by design: this
  # is the checkout state a receiver workflow repairs, and the one where stale hard-coded arguments
  # previously let a legal declaration change generate the wrong root.
  mkgen() {
    local t="$tmp/$1"
    mkdir -p "$t"
    mkskills "$t/.claude/skills" alpha beta
    mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" ".agents/skills"
    printf '%s' "$t"
  }

  echo "generate declaration mode — one reader for source and view roots (.github#1893)"

  t="$(mkgen generate_declared)"
  selftest_generate_case "the declared live root sources the declared view root" ok \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  t="$(mkgen generate_source_conflict)"
  selftest_generate_case "--source cannot override a receiver declaration" "--source and --receiver-proj" \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj" --source "$t/.claude/skills"

  t="$(mkgen generate_roots_conflict)"
  selftest_generate_case "--roots cannot override a receiver declaration" "--roots and --receiver-proj" \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj" --roots ".agents/skills"

  t="$(mkgen generate_missing_live)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" "OMIT" ".agents/skills"
  selftest_generate_case "an undeclared live source property is red on generate" "does not declare <FsggKitSkillRoots>" \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  t="$(mkgen generate_missing_view)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" "OMIT"
  selftest_generate_case "an undeclared view-root property is red on generate" "does not declare <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>" \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # A disposition swap remains legal.  Generate must follow the declaration, rather than continue
  # using a workflow's former --source/--roots pair.
  t="$tmp/generate_swapped"; mkskills "$t/.agents/skills" alpha beta
  mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".agents/skills" ".claude/skills"
  selftest_generate_case "a legal disposition swap drives generate from the new declaration" ok \
    --tree "$t" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  echo

  t="$(mkdecl good)";  mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" ".agents/skills"
  selftest_case "the shipped shape: union is ADR-0011's two" ok \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # A legal ADR-0065 disposition change: same union, roots declared the other way round. Reddening
  # it would make the alarm an obstacle to the contract it protects.
  t="$(mkdecl swapped)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".agents/skills" ".claude/skills"
  selftest_case "legal disposition swap: same union, properties exchanged" ok \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.agents/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # THE REGRESSION THIS LANE EXISTS FOR. Every other kit gate is green on this tree — measured.
  t="$(mkdecl emptied)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" ""
  selftest_case "the view root EMPTIED out of the declaration" "[roots-declaration]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # An absent property must never read as an empty contribution to the union, which would make the
  # deletion the very thing it silently allows.
  t="$(mkdecl deleted)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" "OMIT"
  selftest_case "the view-root property DELETED outright" "[roots-declaration]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # THE CASE THAT SEPARATES "FAILS CLOSED" FROM "HAPPENS TO FAIL THE SET COMPARISON", and it was
  # added because its absence let a mutation SURVIVE. With the view property deleted but the LIVE
  # property alone already naming both roots, the union still equals --expect-roots — so a
  # `read_receiver_proj` that treated an undeclared property as an empty CONTRIBUTION rather than a
  # FAULT reported green here, while the case above stayed red for an unrelated reason and hid it.
  # This is also the pre-retirement shape: both roots materialized, no view root. Passing
  # --receiver-proj asserts this tree IS a view-root adopter, and an adopter that declares no view
  # root has silently un-adopted — which is the regression, not a configuration.
  t="$(mkdecl deleted_but_union_ok)"; mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills;.agents/skills" "OMIT"
  selftest_case "the view property deleted while the LIVE one still spans the union" "[roots-declaration]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # Set equality, not a minimum: ADR-0065 governs ADDING a root exactly as it governs removing one.
  t="$(mkdecl extra)"; mkskills "$t/.codex/skills" alpha beta
  mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills;.codex/skills" ".agents/skills"
  selftest_case "a THIRD root added with no contract migration" "[roots-declaration]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  t="$(mkdecl unreadable)"
  selftest_case "the receiver project cannot be read at all" "[roots-declaration]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/does-not-exist.proj"

  echo
  echo "lane 2 — the view actually resolves (ADR-0067 §8's three silent classes, plus partial)"

  # Declaration correct throughout, so only the view root's SHAPE varies.
  mkview() {
    local t="$tmp/$1"
    mkdir -p "$t/.agents"
    mkskills "$t/.claude/skills" alpha beta
    mkproj "$t/r.proj" ".claude/skills" ".agents/skills"
    printf '%s' "$t"
  }

  t="$(mkview resolving)"; ln -s ../.claude/skills "$t/.agents/skills"
  selftest_case "a resolving symlink view over the same population" ok \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # ADR-0067 §8's HEADLINE class, and the one FS.GG.Audio's alarm reported GREEN for a day.
  t="$(mkview dangling)"; ln -s ../.claude/skills-that-do-not-exist "$t/.agents/skills"
  selftest_case "a DANGLING view-root symlink" "[dangling-root]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # What a COMMITTED symlink degrades to under `git -c core.symlinks=false` (ADR-0067 §6).
  t="$(mkview textfile)"; printf '../.claude/skills' > "$t/.agents/skills"
  selftest_case "a plain TEXT FILE where the view root belongs" "[text-file-root]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # A PARTIAL view. Graded per-skill by IDENTITY, which is strictly stronger than the directory-count
  # comparison all seven hand-copies used: this fires even when the counts match.
  t="$(mkview partial)"; mkskills "$t/.agents/skills" alpha
  selftest_case "a PARTIAL view root (fewer skills than the live root)" "[missing-skill]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  t="$(mkview renamed)"; mkskills "$t/.agents/skills" alpha gamma
  selftest_case "a view root with the RIGHT COUNT and a WRONG NAME" "[missing-skill]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  # Absence is RED by default, and that default is what makes --absent-ok a decision rather than a
  # shrug.
  t="$(mkview absent)"
  selftest_case "an ABSENT view root, with no --absent-ok" "[absent-root]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  t="$(mkview absent_ok)"
  selftest_case "an ABSENT view root, excused by --absent-ok with a reason" ok \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj" \
    --absent-ok "bare-checkout job; the materialize path reds if the generate is ever removed"

  # THE CARVE-OUT IS SCOPED TO VIEW ROOTS. A materialized root's absence is drift and must stay red
  # even when --absent-ok is in force — otherwise the flag quietly excuses the whole root set.
  t="$(mkview live_absent)"; ln -s ../.claude/skills "$t/.agents/skills"
  mv "$t/.claude/skills" "$t/.claude/live"
  selftest_case "an absent LIVE root is STILL red under --absent-ok" "[absent-root]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/live" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj" \
    --absent-ok "this excuse must not reach a materialized root"

  echo
  echo "lane 3 — a view root git TRACKS (adopted from FS.GG.Net's copy)"

  t="$(mkview tracked)"; mkskills "$t/.agents/skills" alpha beta
  git -C "$t" init -q 2>/dev/null || true
  git -C "$t" -c user.email=selftest@fs.gg -c user.name=selftest add -A >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  selftest_case "a COMMITTED view root, resolving perfectly on Linux" "[tracked-view-root]" \
    --tree "$t" --source "$t/.claude/skills" --receiver-proj "$t/r.proj"

  echo
  printf 'skill-view selftest: %d passed, %d failed\n' "$SELFTEST_PASS" "$SELFTEST_FAIL"
  if [ "$SELFTEST_FAIL" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "::error::skill-view: $SELFTEST_FAIL selftest case(s) failed — a lane of 'check' cannot fire, or fires with the wrong class. A gate that cannot fire is not a gate (.github#1611 category D)." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  [ "$SELFTEST_PASS" -gt 0 ] || die "the selftest ran NO cases — that is not a pass (#266)."
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# argv
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CMD="${1:-}"
case "$CMD" in
  generate|check|roots|selftest) shift ;;
  -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
  "") usage >&2; die "no subcommand (expected 'generate', 'check', 'roots' or 'selftest')." ;;
  *) usage >&2; die "unknown subcommand: $CMD (expected 'generate', 'check', 'roots' or 'selftest')." ;;
esac

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --tree)          need_val "$@"; TREE="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --roots)         need_val "$@"; ROOTS_ARG="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --source)        need_val "$@"; SOURCE="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --manifest)      need_val "$@"; MANIFEST="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --mode)          need_val "$@"; MODE="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --receiver-proj) need_val "$@"; RECEIVER_PROJ="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --expect-roots)  need_val "$@"; EXPECT_ROOTS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    # need_val rejects an EMPTY value, which is exactly the guard this flag needs: `--absent-ok ""`
    # is a carve-out with no measured reason behind it, and that is the thing it must not be.
    --absent-ok)     need_val "$@"; ABSENT_OK_WHY="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    -h|--help)       usage; exit 0 ;;
    *) usage >&2; die "unknown argument: $1" ;;
  esac
done

[ -d "$TREE" ] || die "--tree is not a directory: $TREE"
TREE_ABS="$(abs "$TREE")"

if [ "$CMD" = "selftest" ]; then
  do_selftest
  exit 0
fi

if [ -n "$RECEIVER_PROJ" ]; then
  [ -z "$ROOTS_ARG" ] || die "--roots and --receiver-proj both name the root set. The receiver project IS the declaration this lane grades, so overriding it with --roots would grade one set and resolve another."
  if [ "$CMD" = "generate" ]; then
    [ -z "$SOURCE" ] || die "--source and --receiver-proj both name the source root. The receiver project is the declaration the workflow must follow, so overriding it would generate one source into another declaration's view roots."
    [ -z "$EXPECT_ROOTS" ] || die "--expect-roots grades the declaration --receiver-proj reads; generate resolves the declaration rather than grading an extra expectation."
  else
    [ -n "$EXPECT_ROOTS" ] || EXPECT_ROOTS="$DEFAULT_ROOTS"
  fi
  read_receiver_proj "$RECEIVER_PROJ"
  if [ "$CMD" = "generate" ]; then
    [ -z "$MEMBERSHIP_FAULT" ] || die "cannot generate from --receiver-proj '$RECEIVER_PROJ': $MEMBERSHIP_FAULT"
    live_count="$(printf '%s\n' "$LIVE_ROOTS" | wc -w | tr -d ' ')"
    [ "$live_count" = 1 ] || die "--receiver-proj '$RECEIVER_PROJ' declares '$LIVE_ROOTS' as <FsggKitSkillRoots>; generate needs exactly ONE live source root, not $live_count. A multi-source view has no declared identity to copy."
    [ -n "$VIEW_ROOTS" ] || die "--receiver-proj '$RECEIVER_PROJ' declares no <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>; generate has no view root to produce."
    SOURCE="$TREE_ABS/$LIVE_ROOTS"
    ROOTS="$VIEW_ROOTS"
    ROOTS_SRC="$(basename "$RECEIVER_PROJ") (<FsggKitViewSkillRoots>)"
  else
    # A fault leaves ROOTS empty on purpose; do_check reports it as the verdict it is, before the
    # "no roots resolved" die below could turn a real finding into a misconfiguration.
    [ -n "$MEMBERSHIP_FAULT" ] || [ -n "$ROOTS" ] || MEMBERSHIP_FAULT="'$RECEIVER_PROJ' declares both properties but their union is EMPTY."
  fi
else
  [ -z "$ABSENT_OK_WHY" ] || die "--absent-ok applies to VIEW roots, and only --receiver-proj says which roots those are. Excusing an absent root without knowing its disposition would excuse a materialized root's absence too, which is drift."
  [ -z "$EXPECT_ROOTS" ] || die "--expect-roots grades the declaration --receiver-proj reads; on its own it has nothing to compare."
  resolve_roots "$TREE_ABS" "$DEFAULT_ROOTS" "$DEFAULT_LABEL" "$ROOTS_ARG"
  [ -n "$ROOTS" ] || die "no roots resolved — refusing to examine nothing."
fi

case "$CMD" in
  generate) do_generate ;;
  check)    do_check ;;
  roots)    do_roots ;;
esac
